INT. WEDDING CAKE BAKERY - DAY

A tiny shop, glass cases, tiers of fondant in a pastel parade. The air smells of butter and bad decisions.

GERTRUDE POSLUNS (50s, flour to the elbows, apron stained rainbow) frosts a three-tier cake with a palette knife as if defusing a bomb. The door's bell jangles.

                    GERTRUDE
          We're closed.

BARNEY ODUYA (30s, groom, tuxedo jacket, one shoe untied) bursts in, breathless.

                    BARNEY
          Please. Please. I need a cake.

                    GERTRUDE
          You have a cake. You ordered it in March.

                    BARNEY
          I ordered a cake in March. My fiancée ordered another cake in April.

                    GERTRUDE
          I know. I made both.

                    BARNEY
          They're in the same venue. At the same hour.

                    GERTRUDE
          Then you'll have double the dessert.

                    BARNEY
          I'll have double the divorce.

He puts both hands on her glass case. She doesn't look up from the frosting.

                    GERTRUDE
          Which one is yours?

                    BARNEY
          The coconut.

                    GERTRUDE
          Hers?

                    BARNEY
          Also coconut.

                    GERTRUDE
          ^Then they're the same.

                    BARNEY
          ^Then they're the same.

He stops. They look at each other. The bell above the door settles.

                    GERTRUDE
          So it's not a crisis.

                    BARNEY
          It's a crisis of principle.

                    GERTRUDE
          I deal in crises of sugar.

She hands him a bag. Inside, two sprigs of mint.

                    GERTRUDE (CONT'D)
          Put one on each. They won't notice a difference.

                    BARNEY
          Thank you.

                    GERTRUDE
          Go get married.

He bolts. The bell jangles. She resumes frosting. Outside, a car horn.
